  "style": {
    "id": "op-art",
    "name": "Op Art",
    "description": "1960s optical art on a white gallery wall: one large hard-edged panel of pure black-and-white pattern that the eye reads as depth and motion. Vertical stripes whose spacing and black/white duty are warped so a sphere seems to bulge out of the flat wall, and the bulge travels; a clear acetate sheet of rings slides over a ringed panel so moiré bands sweep much faster than the sheet moves; a Vasarely-style grid of checker squares and circles whose size and colour step ring by ring into a dome, then flip into a tunnel, with red and cobalt accents; and a reveal where the camera walks off-axis to show the 'dome' is flat paint on a flat board. Every pattern is analytic in one shader (fwidth antialiasing, stripes >= ~16 px wide, fade to mean grey below Nyquist), camera locked except the reveal, no strobing.",
